  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the wheel pack?",
      "a": "The wheel pack is a stack of separate wheels, one per number in the combination. They turn freely until a pin on the drive cam or the wheel next to it picks them up.",
      "asks": [
        "What is inside a combination lock?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the fence do?",
      "a": "The fence is a bar that rests on the edges of the wheels. It can only drop down when all the gates line up beneath it, and it must drop before the bolt can be pulled back.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the lock know the numbers are right?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the drive cam?",
      "a": "The drive cam is fixed to the dial's spindle and turns whenever you turn the dial. Its pin picks up the first wheel, which picks up the next, and so on.",
      "asks": [
        "What turns the wheels?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do you turn a combination dial back and forth?",
      "a": "Each change of direction leaves one wheel behind at its number. Turning several times first collects all the wheels; then each reversal sets the next one.",
      "asks": [
        "Why turn left then right on a safe?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is a combination lock hard to guess?",
      "a": "Three wheels with 100 numbers each give a million combinations. Trying them all would take far too long.",
      "asks": [
        "How many combinations does a lock have?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A lock with three wheels of 100 numbers each has 1,000,000 possible combinations.",
    "The gate is a notch in each wheel; the fence drops into the gates only when all are lined up."
  ],
